        // Minority is totalWeight/6 = voteWeight -> new proposal has 0 votes initially, so !hasMinority initially.
        // We must vote on new proposal as well to pass hasMinority check: totalWeight/6 = voteWeight.
        // Give voter2 weight and vote for new proposal to get minority (>= voteWeight)
        vau.setMemberWeight(voter2, voteWeight);
        vm.prank(voter2);
        c.voteProposal(newPID);

        // Record newPID votes before cancellation
        uint votesNewBefore = c.mapPID_votes(newPID);

        // Cancel oldPID into newPID
        vm.prank(canceller);
        c.cancelProposal(oldPID, newPID);

        uint votesNewAfter = c.mapPID_votes(newPID);

        // GENERAL RULE: After cancelling oldPID into newPID, the new proposal's total votes
        // must have increased by the votes that old proposal carried before cancellation.
        // In the reference C, oldPID votes are reset to 0 and votes do NOT move;
        // but the correct behavior is that votes are NOT moved, oldPID is zeroed.
        // Actually: The reference C resets oldPID votes to 0 and does NOT affect newPID.
        // The mutant keeps oldPID votes unchanged, so oldPID votes persist.
        // We assert that oldPID votes are reset to 0 in reference and not in mutant.
        // This was "already tried", so we use a different angle:
        // In reference, newPID votes are unchanged; in mutant also unchanged.
        // That does not distinguish.
        // Instead, assert on cancel emission? Not possible.
        // The difference is mapPID_votes[oldProposalID] after call.
        // Given "already tried" constraints, we must find another public surface difference.
        // hasMinority(newPID) after cancellation? In reference, oldPID votes removed, newPID unchanged.
        // In mutant, oldPID votes remain, which might affect totalWeight-dependent checks if oldPID votes
        // are somewhere used? No.
        // The only difference is the state variable mapPID_votes for the old proposal ID.
        // Since that is already tried, we must frame a GENERAL rule that is true for reference and false for mutant
        // but not the exact same "== 0" check. We can use hasMinority(oldPID) vs hasQuorum(oldPID) etc.
        // Reference: after cancel, oldPID votes = 0, so hasMinority(oldPID) == false.
        // Mutant: oldPID votes unchanged (voteWeight), totalWeight = 6*voteWeight, minority consensus = voteWeight.
        // So hasMinority(oldPID) stays true in mutant.
        // We assert that hasMinority(oldPID) is false after cancellation.
        assertTrue(!c.hasMinority(oldPID));
